WEBVTT

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:11.840
<v Ira Glass>In May of last year, about a month after the bombings at the Boston Marathon, there was this weird news story-- maybe you saw this-- about a guy in Florida who was shot by an FBI agent.
<v Ira Glass>去年五月，波士顿马拉松爆炸案发生大约一个月后，出现了一则奇怪的新闻——也许你看到了——一名住在佛罗里达州的男子被FBI探员枪杀。

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:21.600
<v Charlie Rose>We're getting word this morning about a shooting in Orlando, Florida. It involves the FBI and a man who may have had ties to the Boston bombing suspects. Our senior correspondent--
<v Charlie Rose>我们今早接到消息，佛罗里达州奥兰多发生了一起枪击事件。事件涉及FBI和一名可能与波士顿爆炸案嫌疑人有牵连的男子。我们的资深记者——

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:29.950
<v Ira Glass>The shooting victim was a Chechen guy named Ibragim Todashev, and he'd known one of the guys accused in the Boston bombing.
<v Ira Glass>这名枪击案受害者是一名车臣裔男子，名叫易卜拉欣·托达舍夫（Ibragim Todashev），他认识波士顿爆炸案中被指控的一名男子。

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:44.290
<v Man>Ibragim Todashev was a friend of the older brother in that case, Tamerlan Tsarnaev. He was in contact with Tamerlan Tsarnaev. He had been to Boston to visit him, and he was planning a trip--
<v Man>易卜拉欣·托达舍夫是该案中哥哥塔梅尔兰·萨纳耶夫（Tamerlan Tsarnaev）的朋友。他与塔梅尔兰·萨纳耶夫有过联系。他曾去波士顿探望过他，并且正计划去——

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:54.020
<v Ira Glass>Remember that Tamerlan Tsarnaev was the older brother in the Boston Marathon case. He's the one killed in a shootout in Watertown after that crazy car chase. He's usually portrayed as the more radical brother in news reports.
<v Ira Glass>记住，塔梅尔兰·萨纳耶夫是波士顿马拉松案中的哥哥。他是在那场疯狂的汽车追逐战后，在沃特敦的交火中被击毙的那个人。在新闻报道中，他通常被描述为更激进的那个兄弟。

00:00:54.020 --> 00:01:08.870
<v Ira Glass>That's who the Florida guy was buddies with. And the Florida guy was shot in his own apartment by the FBI seven times during an interview they were doing with him. The FBI said almost nothing about this. But of course, there were leaks.
<v Ira Glass>佛罗里达那名男子就是他的哥们儿。而佛罗里达那名男子在接受FBI审讯时，在自己的公寓里被FBI连开七枪打死。FBI对此几乎只字未提。但当然，还是有一些泄密消息传出。

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:14.170
<v Man>And officials tell us that the man attacked the FBI agent, who then shot and killed him.
<v Man>官员们告诉我们，该男子袭击了FBI探员，探员随后将其开枪击毙。

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:21.710
<v Ira Glass>Some reports said that he came at the agent with a knife. Others said it was a pole. Others said a broomstick, maybe a sword.
<v Ira Glass>有的报道说他拿着刀冲向探员。有的说是根棍子。还有的说是扫帚柄，甚至可能是把剑。

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:54.350
<v Ira Glass>And the story was leaked to the press by law enforcement was that before the FBI shot this guy, Ibrahim Todashev, he confessed to something. He implicated himself and the alleged Boston bomber in a crime, a gruesome crime, a triple murder of three drug dealers who were killed gangland style in a Boston suburb two years earlier, long before the marathon bombings. According to the leaks, this guy in Florida was just about to write out a confession when he freaked out, attacked a federal agent, and they had to shoot him to death.
<v Ira Glass>执法部门泄露给媒体的故事是：在FBI击毙易卜拉欣·托达舍夫之前，他供认了一些事情。他指认自己和所谓的波士顿爆炸案嫌疑人共同犯下了一桩罪行，一桩骇人听闻的罪行——两年前在波士顿郊区，也就是马拉松爆炸案发生前很久，有三名毒贩以黑帮手法被杀。根据泄密消息，这名身在佛罗里达的男子本打算写下供词，却突然发狂，袭击了一名联邦探员，导致他们不得不将他击毙。

00:01:54.350 --> 00:02:06.040
<v Ira Glass>Weird, right? The FBI kills somebody in his own home and then says, sure, we killed him. But, you know, he was a murderer. He confessed to it. OK, well, we don't have a recording or a signed confession, but believe us, he really did it.
<v Ira Glass>很奇怪，对吧？FBI在他家里杀了一个人，然后说，没错，是我们杀了他。但是，你知道的，他是个杀人犯。他供认了。好吧，我们没有录音，也没有签署的供词，但请相信我们，他确实干了。

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:33.390
<v Ira Glass>As one editorial put it in a suburban Massachusetts paper, quote, "When a key witness to a serious crime is killed during questioning, people deserve to know why." When prosecutors say he revealed a critical fact before he was killed, people expect proof. The father of Ibragim Todashev-- the guy the FBI killed in Florida-- held a press conference calling for answers, and he pointed out it was hard to understand why federal agents would need to actually kill his son. He spoke in Russian.
<v Ira Glass>正如马萨诸塞州一家郊区报纸的社论所言：“当严重罪行的关键证人在审讯中被杀时，人们有权知道原因。”当检察官声称他在被杀前透露了关键事实时，人们期待证据。易卜拉欣·托达舍夫的父亲——也就是被FBI在佛罗里达击毙的那个人——举行了新闻发布会要求给出答案，他指出很难理解联邦探员为何非要杀掉他的儿子。他用俄语发言。

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:48.920
<v Translator>Even if he lost his temper and became violent, they could have restrained him or wounded him, shoot him in the leg or the arm or the shoulder. But what happened was murder. Maybe my son knew something the police didn't want to come out, and they killed him to keep him silent.
<v Translator>即使他发火并变得暴力，他们也可以制服他或者打伤他，朝他的腿、手臂或肩膀开枪。但发生的事是谋杀。也许我儿子知道一些警察不想让外界知道的事情，于是他们杀了他以求灭口。

00:02:48.920 --> 00:03:09.830
<v Ira Glass>Theories sprang up. On the more conspiracy-ish side of things, there had already been persistent rumors that the alleged Boston bomber, Tamerlan Tsarnaev, had been an FBI informant. Maybe this guy in Florida knew something about that and they wanted to shut him up. These theories were especially rife in Boston, where the FBI has colluded with its criminal informants in the past.
<v Ira Glass>各种理论层出不穷。在阴谋论那一面，一直有传言称波士顿爆炸案嫌疑人塔梅尔兰·萨纳耶夫曾是FBI的线人。也许佛罗里达这名男子知道一些内情，而他们想让他闭嘴。这些理论在波士顿尤为盛行，因为那里曾发生过FBI与其线人勾结的丑闻。

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:37.700
<v Ira Glass>At the very least, people wondered if the FBI was being so silent about the incident because it didn't want to admit its own blunders during the interrogation, blunders that led them to shoot a man seven times. The Boston Globe, Boston Herald, and dozens of other newspapers have called for an explanation of how and why this happened. The Washington Post declared, "The last thing the US government needs is to fuel wild conspiracy theories. The Obama administration must move heaven and earth to get to the bottom of what happened and make it public, quickly."
<v Ira Glass>至少，人们怀疑FBI之所以对这起事件保持沉默，是因为不想承认在审讯过程中犯下的拙劣错误，正是这些错误导致他们向一个人连开了七枪。《波士顿环球报》、《波士顿先驱报》和数十家其他报纸都呼吁解释这件事发生的过程和原因。《华盛顿邮报》宣称：“美国政府最不需要的就是助长疯狂的阴谋论。奥巴马政府必须竭尽全力查明真相，并迅速向公众公布。”

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:53.360
<v Ira Glass>And the FBI promised to clear the air. They promised an investigation into the shooting. That was last May. Since then, officials keep saying, essentially, it's coming, it's coming. Now it has been nine months, and still, nothing. Nine months.
<v Ira Glass>FBI承诺会澄清事实。他们承诺对枪击事件展开调查。那是去年五月的事。从那以后，官员们一直在说，本质上就是，“调查快出来了，快出来了”。现在已经九个月了，仍然什么都没有。九个月了。

00:03:53.360 --> 00:04:13.100
<v Ira Glass>At this point, the mother of one of the men killed in that triple murder now agrees with the dad you heard-- the dad whose son supposedly committed the murder, murdered her son-- that things don't seem to add up. This mom, Bellie Hacker, wonders why would the FBI kill the one person who could shed light on who murdered her kid?
<v Ira Glass>目前，那起三尸命案中一名遇害者的母亲现在也认同你们听到的那位父亲的说法——那位儿子的死被认为杀害了她儿子的父亲——事情似乎有些讲不通。这位母亲，贝莉·哈克（Bellie Hacker），想知道为什么FBI要杀掉唯一能揭开谁杀害她孩子真相的人？

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:27.150
<v Bellie Hacker>I know there's some sort of a cover up somewhere. I don't know what it is, and I don't have any theories about it. But it's too strange.
<v Bellie Hacker>我知道某个地方肯定有掩盖真相的行为。我不知道具体是什么，我也没有任何理论。但这一切太奇怪了。

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:32.390
<v Bill Keating>A full and swift release of information would clear the air about that.
<v Bill Keating>全面且迅速地发布信息会澄清对此事的疑虑。

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:56.370
<v Ira Glass>Massachusetts congressman Bill Keating sits on the Homeland Security committee, and he says that in general, when it comes to anything related to the Boston bombing, the FBI is peculiarly unforthcoming, much worse than any other law enforcement agencies when giving out information. And he says investigations into police shootings just do not have to take this long. He knows this because he's been involved with them himself back when he was a district attorney.
<v Ira Glass>马萨诸塞州众议员比尔·基廷（Bill Keating）是国土安全委员会成员，他表示，总体而言，凡是与波士顿爆炸案相关的任何事情，FBI都异常地不配合，在提供信息方面远比其他执法机构差劲。他还表示，对警察枪击事件的调查根本不需要耗费这么长时间。他对此很清楚，因为他曾担任过地方检察官，亲自参与过此类调查。

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.070
<v Bill Keating>I think the longest one we were involved in was maybe two months.
<v Bill Keating>我想我们参与过的最长的调查可能也就两个月。

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.170
<v Ira Glass>Versus this case.
<v Ira Glass>相比于此案。

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:22.100
<v Bill Keating>In this instance, there was a crime scene that amounted to a room and just a few individuals, so it's something that should have taken short order. And even though there's an extensive report making vetting that goes on, that delay now continues eight or nine months later. And that is puzzling.
<v Bill Keating>在这种情况下，犯罪现场不过是一个房间和几个人，所以这应该很快就能解决。尽管有广泛的报告审查流程，但这种延误在八九个月后仍在继续。这很令人困惑。

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:27.570
<v Ira Glass>From the outside, it just makes it look so suspicious. Do you know what I mean?
<v Ira Glass>从外部看，这显得非常可疑。你明白我的意思吗？

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:31.860
<v Bill Keating>Well, that's what happens when, you know, delays occur.
<v Bill Keating>嗯，你知道，当延误发生时，情况就是这样。

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:50.330
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, we dive into this mystery. There's a triple murder in Massachusetts linked to the alleged Boston bomber and this other guy in Florida. Both of those men are dead-- the one in Florida in circumstances that are still very unclear. And as you'll hear, the deeper that we dug into these questions, the weirder the story got.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们将深入探讨这个谜团。马萨诸塞州发生了一起涉及所谓波士顿爆炸案嫌疑人和佛罗里达那名男子的三尸命案。这两个人都死了——佛罗里达那一个死时的情形依然非常不清楚。正如你将会听到的，我们对这些问题挖掘得越深，故事就变得越奇怪。

00:05:50.330 --> 00:06:16.860
<v Ira Glass>The police and FBI were doing all kinds of things that just did not seem to make much sense. We're going to bring you the facts that we've been able to find. We're going to tell you the ones that we could not find, and then at the end of the hour, our best guess as to what we think might have happened. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>警察和FBI做的所有事情似乎都毫无道理。我们将向你提供我们所能发现的事实。我们也会告诉你那些我们无法发现的信息，然后在节目结束时，我们将给出我们对可能发生的事情的最佳猜测。来自芝加哥WBEZ，这是《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:32.060
<v Ira Glass>Well, Susan Zalkind is a reporter who lives in Boston, and she has spent the last six months looking into all this for Boston magazine and for us, and also for herself. She has a personal connection to what happened, this triple murder, the one that the guy in Florida supposedly is connected to.
<v Ira Glass>苏珊·扎尔金德（Susan Zalkind）是一位住在波士顿的记者，过去六个月她一直在为《波士顿》杂志、为我们，也为了她自己调查这一切。她与这件事——那起佛罗里达男子据称与之有关的三尸命案——有着私人联系。

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:43.290
<v Ira Glass>Her connection is that she was friends with one of the victims in that original crime-- one of the three drug dealers who was killed, Eric Weissman. So here's Susan.
<v Ira Glass>她的联系是，她是那起原始罪案的受害者之一的朋友——那三名被杀毒贩之一，埃里克·韦斯曼（Eric Weissman）。下面请听苏珊的讲述。

00:06:43.290 --> 00:07:13.060
<v Susan Zalkind>I met Eric five years before he was killed. It was the summer after my freshman year in college. I was 19 and he was 26, and we hung out a few times that summer, usually driving around aimlessly in his car, listening to music, talking, and smoking pot. He had wire-rimmed nerd glasses and a Jewfro, and honestly, he was just really sweet. He sold high-end weed, flew to Amsterdam regularly for special seeds, and talked about selling pot as if it were a community service.
<v Susan Zalkind>我在埃里克被杀前五年认识了他。那是大学一年级后的夏天。我19岁，他26岁，那个夏天我们经常聚在一起，通常是漫无目的地开车兜风、听音乐、聊天、抽大麻。他戴着细框眼镜，留着犹太式蓬松卷发，老实说，他真的很贴心。他卖高档大麻，经常飞往阿姆斯特丹买特殊种子，谈起卖大麻时就好像是在从事社区服务一样。

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:21.580
<v Susan Zalkind>Over time, I stopped smoking weed. We grew apart. The last time I spoke to him was eight months before he was killed.
<v Susan Zalkind>随着时间推移，我不再抽大麻了。我们渐行渐远。我最后一次和他说话是在他被杀前八个月。

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:41.850
<v Susan Zalkind>My dad's a criminal defense attorney, and Eric wanted his number to hire him. Police had raided his apartment. A few years earlier, he told me he only sold pot, but police also seized coke and OxyContin. He sounded scared. I told him it would be OK.
<v Susan Zalkind>我父亲是刑事辩护律师，埃里克想拿到他的电话号码以便聘请他。警察突袭了他的公寓。几年前他告诉我他只卖大麻，但警察还搜出了可卡因和奥施康定。他听起来很害怕。我告诉他会没事的。

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:55.580
<v Gary Leone>Approximately 2:30 PM today, the Waltham police responded to this scene, a-- sorry-- very graphic crime scene. There are three dead bodies in the apartment. This is a fluid, ongoing investigation.
<v Gary Leone>今天下午大约2点30分，沃尔瑟姆（Waltham）警方赶到了这个现场，一个……抱歉……非常血腥的犯罪现场。公寓里有三具尸体。这是一个流动的、正在进行的调查。

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:19.430
<v Susan Zalkind>The day Eric's body was discovered, Middlesex County District Attorney Gerry Leone faced a scrum of reporters outside the crime scene in Waltham, a suburb of Boston. Leone said he couldn't release many details, not before they got in touch with the victims' families. He wouldn't even confirm the victims' genders or their approximate ages. There wasn't a lot of information, except for this, which seemed very specific.
<v Susan Zalkind>埃里克的尸体被发现的那天，米德尔塞克斯县地方检察官格里·利昂（Gerry Leone）在沃尔瑟姆——波士顿的一个郊区——的犯罪现场外面对一大群记者。利昂说他不能透露太多细节，在联系上受害者家属之前不能透露。他甚至不愿确认受害者的性别或大概年龄。除了这一点看起来非常具体的信息外，没有太多其他消息。

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:32.670
<v Gary Leone>It does look like the assailants and the decedents did know each other. We have no evidence of a break in the apartment, and we have other indicia that the decedents and the assailants were known to each other.
<v Gary Leone>看起来袭击者和死者确实互相认识。我们没有发现公寓有被强行闯入的迹象，还有其他迹象表明死者和袭击者相互认识。

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:36.280
<v Reporter>You're saying assailants, plural. You mean you're thinking that more than one person is responsible?
<v Reporter>你说的是“袭击者”，复数。你是说你认为不止一个人对此负责？

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:43.770
<v Gary Leone>We're not sure at this time. We know there were at least two people who are not in that apartment now who were there earlier.
<v Gary Leone>目前我们还不确定。我们知道至少有两个人曾在那间公寓，但现在已不在那里了。

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:58.430
<v Susan Zalkind>It was later revealed that the killers slit their throats. The three bodies lay face down, heads turned to the side, each in a neat pool of blood. Two of the bodies had about a pound and a half of marijuana dumped on them. The scene didn't scan like a robbery.
<v Susan Zalkind>后来披露，杀手割断了他们的喉咙。三具尸体面部朝下，头部转向一侧，每一具都躺在整齐的血泊中。其中两具尸体上被倾倒了大约一磅半的大麻。现场看起来不像抢劫。

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:16.790
<v Susan Zalkind>There were eight and a half pounds of pot left in bags and glass jars and $5,000 lying next to the bodies. They were killed sometime after 7:30 on the night of September 11, 2011.
<v Susan Zalkind>现场留下了八磅半装在袋子和玻璃罐里的大麻，还有5000美元现金散落在尸体旁边。他们是在2011年9月11日晚上7点30分之后被杀的。

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:44.600
<v Susan Zalkind>Weeks passed, and months. My friends and I would convene nightly meetings huddled in our apartments and run through all the possibilities of who could have killed our friend. But there didn't seem to be any leads, and certainly no arrests. I didn't hear much more about the case, really, until a year and a half later, after the Boston Marathon bombings, when news reports said police and FBI were beginning to look into the connection between the alleged bomber, Tamerlan Tsarnaev, and the Waltham murders.
<v Susan Zalkind>几周、几个月过去了。我和朋友们每晚都会聚在我们公寓里，讨论谁可能杀了我们的朋友。但似乎没有任何线索，当然也没有人被捕。我确实没有听到关于这个案子的更多消息，直到一年半后，在波士顿马拉松爆炸案发生之后，新闻报道称警方和FBI开始调查所谓爆炸案嫌疑人塔梅尔兰·萨纳耶夫与沃尔瑟姆谋杀案之间的联系。

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:55.000
<v Susan Zalkind>Tamerlan had been close friends with one of the three murder victims, a guy named Brendan Mess. But after the murders, Tamerlan had acted suspiciously, Didn't go to Brendan's funeral.
<v Susan Zalkind>塔梅尔兰曾与三名受害者之一的布伦丹·梅斯（Brendan Mess）是密友。但谋杀案发生后，塔梅尔兰表现得很可疑，他没去参加布伦丹的葬礼。

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:28.190
<v Susan Zalkind>I talked to one guy who said he went up to Tamerlan to offer his condolences about Brendan, and Tamerlan literally laughed it off. People actually told investigators to look into Tamerlan Tsarnaev back in 2011, right after the murders, but police never followed up. After the marathon bombings, there were rumblings of a scandal about all this. If police had questioned Tamerlan back in 2011, is it possible he'd be in prison instead of placing two homemade bombs at the finish line?
<v Susan Zalkind>我和一个家伙谈过，他说他曾走近塔梅尔兰对他表示对布伦丹的哀悼，结果塔梅尔兰竟然当众大笑。实际上在2011年谋杀案刚发生后，就有人告诉调查人员去调查塔梅尔兰·萨纳耶夫，但警方从未跟进。马拉松爆炸案后，关于这一切的丑闻传言四起。如果警察在2011年就审问了塔梅尔兰，他是否可能已经待在监狱里，而不是在终点线放置了两枚自制炸弹？

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:49.570
<v Susan Zalkind>But then pretty quickly came the same news reports you heard at the beginning of the show, that some guy in Florida named Ibragim Todashev supposedly admitted that he'd been involved in the Waltham murders, and that Tamerlan had been with him. There was no written confession. There was no reporting. And then Todashev was shot by an FBI agent.
<v Susan Zalkind>但随后很快就出现了你们在节目开始时听到的同样的新闻报道，称佛罗里达一个名叫易卜拉欣·托达舍夫的家伙据称承认自己参与了沃尔瑟姆谋杀案，而且塔梅尔兰当时也在场。没有书面供词。没有报道细节。然后托达舍夫就被FBI探员枪杀了。

00:10:49.570 --> 00:11:02.620
<v Susan Zalkind>I had no idea what to make of it. A year and a half had gone by with no news at all, and now, suddenly, they had found the killers, and both of them are dead? Should I really believe that these two guys murdered my friend?
<v Susan Zalkind>我不知道该怎么理解这一切。一年半过去了，一点消息都没有，现在突然间他们找到了杀手，而且这两个人都死了？我真的应该相信这两个人谋杀了我的朋友吗？

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:20.170
<v Susan Zalkind>So I start to look into everything I could learn about the guy in Florida, Ibragim Todashev. I talked to anyone I could reach who knew him-- friends, family, former coworkers-- and asked them all what kind of guy Ibragim Todashev was. But it seemed like he was a hard person to get a read on.
<v Susan Zalkind>所以我开始调查关于佛罗里达那个叫易卜拉欣·托达舍夫的人所能了解的一切。我联系了所有能联系到的认识他的人——朋友、家人、前同事——询问他们易卜拉欣·托达舍夫是什么样的人。但看起来他很难让人摸透。

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:26.480
<v Man>Well, OK, the one thing I noticed about him, he never smiled in his pictures or anything like that.
<v Man>嗯，好吧，关于他我注意到的唯一一件事是，他在照片里从来不笑，或其他类似的情况。

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:33.580
<v Man>I used to see him at the mosque. He was a good guy, nice guy. Like, you know, doing his prayers, and all that.
<v Man>我以前在清真寺见过他。他是个好人，很好的人。就像，你知道，做祷告之类的。

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:38.650
<v Man>Ibragim-- I never liked that dude. I'm gonna be honest. He was, like, a douchebag.
<v Man>易卜拉欣——我从来不喜欢那家伙。我要说实话。他就像个混蛋。

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:42.220
<v Man>To be honest, this guy seems like he had a temper.
<v Man>老实说，这家伙似乎脾气很暴躁。

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:49.070
<v Woman>He was-- I can't speak how he was with somebody else, but with me, he was grateful, thoughtful husband.
<v Woman>他是——我不确定他跟别人是怎么相处的，但对我来说，他是个懂得感恩、体贴的丈夫。

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:53.430
<v Man>He was actually, if you get to know him, he was a pretty good guy.
<v Man>其实，如果你了解他，他是个相当不错的人。

00:11:53.430 --> 00:12:12.590
<v Susan Zalkind>I learned Ibragim was religious and prayed regularly, but he also went to clubs and hit on women. He liked to hang out with a close-knit group of Chechen friends, and he was a fighter. He trained seriously in mixed martial arts, a sport where boxing, wrestling, kickboxing and jujitsu are all mixed together. He wanted to go pro.
<v Susan Zalkind>我了解到易卜拉欣很虔诚，经常祷告，但他也会去夜店搭讪女性。他喜欢和一群关系紧密的车臣朋友待在一起，而且他是个格斗选手。他曾认真地训练综合格斗，这是一种拳击、摔跤、自由搏击和柔术混合在一起的运动。他想成为职业选手。

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:36.970
<v Susan Zalkind>He'd emigrated from Chechnya to Boston in 2008, lived there for a few years, worked a few odd jobs, got married, got separated, spent some time in Atlanta, and by the end of 2011, a few months after the murders in Waltham, he had moved to Florida. But one detail caught my attention. At the time of his death, Ibragim had been living with a woman named Tatiana Gruzdeva.
<v Susan Zalkind>他2008年从车臣移民到波士顿，在那里住了几年，打了几份零工，结了婚，又分居了，在亚特兰大待了一段时间，到2011年底，也就是沃尔瑟姆谋杀案发生几个月后，他搬到了佛罗里达。但有一个细节引起了我的注意。在他去世时，易卜拉欣正与一名叫塔蒂亚娜·格鲁兹德娃（Tatiana Gruzdeva）的女子同居。

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:55.940
<v Susan Zalkind>The Boston Globe wrote that Tatiana had been detained for having an expired visa, and was on her way to being deported to Moscow. But later, they reported that Tatiana had been mysteriously released, and was still living in Florida. I found her the way anyone finds anybody, on Facebook.
<v Susan Zalkind>《波士顿环球报》报道称，塔蒂亚娜因签证过期被拘留，即将被遣返回莫斯科。但后来他们报道说，塔蒂亚娜被神秘地释放了，而且仍住在佛罗里达。我通过每个人找人的方式找到了她——Facebook。

00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:08.430
<v Susan Zalkind>A slight 19-year-old with bleach blond hair and huge green eyes, she posted a lot of selfies. Impulsively, I sent her a friend request. It didn't surprise me that she didn't respond.
<v Susan Zalkind>她是个19岁的瘦弱女孩，染着一头漂白过的金发，有着巨大的绿眼睛，她发了很多自拍。我冲动之下给她发了好友请求。她没有回复，我并不感到惊讶。

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:19.710
<v Susan Zalkind>Months went by. Then, one night in September, while I was working on my computer in my living room, a little alert appeared on my screen. Tatiana accepted my Facebook request.
<v Susan Zalkind>几个月过去了。然后，九月的一个晚上，当我在客厅用电脑工作时，屏幕上弹出了一个提醒。塔蒂亚娜接受了我的Facebook好友请求。

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:38.120
<v Susan Zalkind>We started messaging. Soon we switched to the phone. The first time I talked to Tatiana, I didn't know it was going to be doing a radio story. I don't have an audio recording, but I took notes, and I tried to get down as much of her rapid fire, Russian accented voice as possible. She told me she'd only known Ibragim for six months.
<v Susan Zalkind>我们开始互发信息。很快就转为电话联系。第一次和塔蒂亚娜谈话时，我不知道这会变成一个广播报道。我没有录音，但我做了笔记，并尽量记录下她那种语速极快、带着俄语口音的声音。她告诉我她认识易卜拉欣才六个月。

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:52.500
<v Susan Zalkind>They began as roommates. First it was just friends, she told me, and after, "we started having relationship, and we were sleeping together like boyfriend and girlfriend."
<v Susan Zalkind>他们起初是室友。她说，最初只是朋友，后来，“我们开始谈恋爱，就像男朋友和女朋友一样住在一起。”

00:13:52.500 --> 00:14:13.050
<v Susan Zalkind>Tatiana told me the night Ibragim was shot was not the first time he talked to the FBI. I think most people had the impression that the FBI showed up, started asking Ibragim about a murder, he freaked out, and they shot him. In fact, though, both Tatiana and Ibragim had been cooperating with them for more than a month.
<v Susan Zalkind>塔蒂亚娜告诉我，易卜拉欣被枪杀的那天晚上并不是他第一次和FBI谈话。我想大多数人的印象是FBI突然找上门，开始询问易卜拉欣关于谋杀案的事，他吓坏了，于是他们就开枪打死了他。但事实上，塔蒂亚娜和易卜拉欣与他们配合已经超过一个月了。

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:33.460
<v Susan Zalkind>The relationship with the FBI had become almost cordial, businesslike. Agents contacted the couple regularly on the phone, visited their home, and called them into the FBI offices for more questioning. I wanted to know what they were asking about. Did it seem like the FBI had connected Ibragim to the Waltham murders? Were they asking about them?
<v Susan Zalkind>这种与FBI的关系已经变得近乎客气、公事公办。探员经常给这对情侣打电话、去他们家拜访，并把他们叫到FBI办公室接受更多询问。我想知道他们都在问些什么。FBI似乎把易卜拉欣和沃尔瑟姆谋杀案联系起来了吗？他们在询问这起案子吗？

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:55.260
<v Susan Zalkind>Tatiana said no. Waltham didn't come up, as far as she knew. She said their questions for Ibragim were mostly about terrorism, the Boston bombing, and his relationship with Tamerlan Tsarnaev. Ibragim admitted he was friends with Tamerlan back when he lived in Boston. She said the agents asked about a phone call Tamerlan made to Ibragim about a month before the marathon bombing.
<v Susan Zalkind>塔蒂亚娜说没有。据她所知，沃尔瑟姆案从未提起过。她说他们对易卜拉欣的询问大多是关于恐怖主义、波士顿爆炸案以及他与塔梅尔兰·萨纳耶夫的关系。易卜拉欣承认他在波士顿居住时曾与塔梅尔兰是朋友。她说探员们询问了塔梅尔兰在马拉松爆炸案发生前大约一个月给易卜拉欣打过的一个电话。

00:14:55.260 --> 00:15:09.950
<v Susan Zalkind>Ibragim told them the call was nothing important, that Tamerlan was just checking up on Ibragim after he had surgery on his knee. But later, Ibragim deleted the call from his phone history. When agents asked him why, Ibragim told them he was scared.
<v Susan Zalkind>易卜拉欣告诉他们那通电话没什么重要的，只是塔梅尔兰在他做完膝盖手术后关心一下他的情况。但后来，易卜拉欣从他的通话记录中删除了那个电话。当探员问他原因时，易卜拉欣告诉他们他很害怕。

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:25.260
<v Susan Zalkind>Then, three weeks into the interviews, Tatiana was in the lobby of the FBI office, waiting for Ibragim to be done with another round of questioning, and an agent asked her to come with him. It's fine, she says they told her. Ibragim knows about it.
<v Susan Zalkind>接着，在审讯进行了三周后，塔蒂亚娜在FBI办公室的大厅里等易卜拉欣结束另一轮审讯，一名探员要求她跟他走一趟。她说他们告诉她没关系的，易卜拉欣知道这件事。

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:33.630
<v Susan Zalkind>She went to a room with them. They asked her a bunch of familiar questions about the marathon bombing. And then they brought up something new.
<v Susan Zalkind>她和他们进了房间。他们问了她一连串关于马拉松爆炸案的老问题。然后他们提出了一个新的问题。

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:59.380
<v Susan Zalkind>We think he did something else before, in Boston, 2011, with a knife, Tatiana says they told her. It was the first time Tatiana had ever heard about the Waltham triple murder. She told the agents she didn't believe that Ibragim had anything to do with it. She said the agents then asked her if she'd be willing to let them know if Ibragim started doing anything suspicious, basically to inform on him.
<v Susan Zalkind>塔蒂亚娜说他们告诉她：“我们认为他在2011年之前在波士顿用刀做过别的事。”那是塔蒂亚娜第一次听说沃尔瑟姆三尸命案。她告诉探员她不相信易卜拉欣与此有任何关系。她说探员随后问她如果易卜拉欣开始做任何可疑的事情，她是否愿意告诉他们，基本上就是让她当告密者。

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:16.810
<v Susan Zalkind>She says she said no, because he wasn't doing anything, and she didn't know anything. So the agents called immigration, and they locked her up. She was in the States on an expired student visa.
<v Susan Zalkind>她说她拒绝了，因为他没做任何事，而且她什么都不知道。于是探员给移民局打了电话，把她关了起来。她是以过期的学生签证留在美国的。

00:16:16.810 --> 00:16:33.630
<v Susan Zalkind>Tatiana was held in an immigration detention facility for a week. The day after Ibragim was shot, she was transferred to the Glades County Detention Center and placed in a medical unit. She asked officers why she was being moved, and they told her they'd tell her in the morning.
<v Susan Zalkind>塔蒂亚娜在移民拘留所被关了一周。在易卜拉欣被枪杀的第二天，她被转移到格莱兹县拘留中心，并被安置在医疗单元。她问官员为什么要转移她，他们告诉她早上会告诉她原因。

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:47.550
<v Susan Zalkind>The next day, she learned the truth. Her boyfriend was dead. She didn't return home for another 11 weeks, to the apartment she'd once shared with Ibragim.
<v Susan Zalkind>第二天，她得知了真相。她的男朋友死了。她在那之后的11周才回到那个她曾与易卜拉欣共享的公寓。

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:50.070
<v Susan Zalkind>I'm just gonna leave the recorder running.
<v Susan Zalkind>我准备让录音机一直开着。

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:50.490
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>All right.
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>好。

00:16:50.490 --> 00:17:08.970
<v Susan Zalkind>Three days after I talked to Tatiana on the phone, I flew down to Orlando to meet with her in person. And this time, I brought a tape recorder. But sitting in the tiny living room of her apartment, the room where Ibragim had been killed, she was much more reluctant to talk about Ibragim or the FBI or, really, much of anything.
<v Susan Zalkind>我和塔蒂亚娜通完电话三天后，我飞往奥兰多亲自见她。这次，我带了录音机。但坐在她那狭小的公寓客厅里，也就是易卜拉欣被杀的那个房间，她对谈论易卜拉欣、FBI，或者说谈论任何事情都变得更加抗拒。

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:15.400
<v Susan Zalkind>What first made you fall in love with Ibragim?
<v Susan Zalkind>是什么让你第一次爱上易卜拉欣的？

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:18.210
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>I really don't want to do this.
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>我真的不想这么做。

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:38.920
<v Susan Zalkind>One reason Tatiana was scared, why she responded to my Facebook request at all weeks after I sent it, was because earlier that same day, she and her friend had been driving when they got pulled over by six or seven unmarked police cars and her friend was taken away. She didn't know who had taken him or why, but she was afraid she might be next.
<v Susan Zalkind>塔蒂亚娜感到害怕的一个原因，也是为什么她在发完请求几周后才回复我的Facebook请求，是因为就在同一天早些时候，她和朋友开车时被六七辆便衣警车拦下，她的朋友被带走了。她不知道是谁带走了他或原因是什么，但她很担心自己可能是下一个。

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:44.510
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>I know, but I'm really scared, you know, about just--
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>我知道，但我真的很害怕，你知道，关于——

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:50.530
<v Susan Zalkind>I understand that you're just scared, like, in general.
<v Susan Zalkind>我理解你只是很害怕，大概是这样。

00:17:50.530 --> 00:17:55.820
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>Because after this jail, and right after, I'm really scared.
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>因为在坐牢之后，而且紧接着发生这些，我真的很害怕。

00:17:55.820 --> 00:18:16.720
<v Susan Zalkind>It was also weird being in that apartment with a 19-year-old girl whose bed was covered with stuffed animals. She showed me a video she'd taken with her phone of her and Ibragim in a Hallmark type store on Valentine's Day. She's giggling and trying to get him to put on a silly hat. She seemed to be like any other teenage girl with a standoffish boyfriend.
<v Susan Zalkind>待在那间公寓里也感觉很奇怪，那儿住着一个床上铺满毛绒玩具的19岁女孩。她给我看了一段她用手机拍的视频，那是情人节时她和易卜拉欣在一家礼品店里的场景。她咯咯笑着，试图让他戴上一顶滑稽的帽子。她看起来就像任何其他有一个高冷男朋友的少女一样。

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:22.680
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>[SPEAKING FOREIGN LANGUAGE]
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>（讲外语）

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:33.290
<v Susan Zalkind>She knew about my connection to the Waltham murders. She knew that her boyfriend is thought to be the person who killed my friend. But she didn't think it was even slightly possible he did it.
<v Susan Zalkind>她知道我和沃尔瑟姆谋杀案的联系。她知道她的男朋友被认为是那个杀害我朋友的人。但她认为他做了这件事的可能性甚至微乎其微。

00:18:33.290 --> 00:18:52.720
<v Susan Zalkind>I tried not to push her on that point, but it was practically all I could think about. Did her dead boyfriend slit my friend's throat? Instead, I told her I thought it was wrong of the FBI to kill Ibragim, even if he was a murderer. After a few hours of talking, I decided to leave Tatiana alone.
<v Susan Zalkind>我尽量不去在那一点上逼迫她，但那几乎是我脑子里唯一能想到的事。她死去的男朋友割断了我朋友的喉咙吗？相反，我告诉她，我认为即使易卜拉欣是个杀人犯，FBI杀死他也是不对的。谈了几个小时后，我决定让塔蒂亚娜独自待着。

00:18:52.720 --> 00:19:00.870
<v Susan Zalkind>Well, keep in touch with me. If something happens, you know, and you-- keep in touch with me. Let me know what's going on.
<v Susan Zalkind>好吧，保持联系。如果发生什么事，你知道，还有你——跟我保持联系。让我知道情况进展。

00:19:00.870 --> 00:19:03.280
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>All right. I hope nothing.
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>好的。希望什么都不会发生。

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:07.410
<v Susan Zalkind>Hopefully nothing, but-- nothing bad's going to ever happen to you, but--
<v Susan Zalkind>希望如此，但是——没什么坏事会发生在你身上，但是——

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:10.380
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>I hope. I hope not, because it's already a lot.
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>我希望。我希望不要，因为这已经够多了。

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:15.180
<v Susan Zalkind>Uh-huh.
<v Susan Zalkind>嗯。

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:15.660
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>[INAUDIBLE].
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>（听不清）

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:26.350
<v Susan Zalkind>OK, OK, Tatiana, who-- who-- who is the immigration officer who told you that you're here because of an interview you gave to me?
<v Susan Zalkind>好的，好的，塔蒂亚娜，那个告诉你是由于接受了我采访才被关在这里的移民局官员是谁？

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:27.750
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>[INAUDIBLE].
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>（听不清）

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:29.580
<v Susan Zalkind>Give me the--
<v Susan Zalkind>告诉我——

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:42.560
<v Ira Glass>A week after I left Orlando, Tatiana called me from an immigration detention facility. She told me because she did that interview with me, and I wrote a story about it for Boston magazine's website, she was now being deported.
<v Ira Glass>我离开奥兰多一周后，塔蒂亚娜从移民拘留所打来电话。她告诉我，因为她接受了我的采访，而我为《波士顿》杂志网站写了一篇相关报道，她现在要被遣返了。

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:48.250
<v Susan Zalkind>I can't believe that that's happened, though. And if that's the only reason, it's really concerning.
<v Susan Zalkind>我简直不敢相信这发生了。如果这是唯一的原因，那真的很令人不安。

00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:55.080
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>Right? It's stupid, right? It's crazy! I can't believe it's true, but-- because of this interview.
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>对吧？很蠢，对吧？太疯狂了！我不敢相信这是真的，但是——是因为这次采访。

00:19:55.080 --> 00:20:06.760
<v Susan Zalkind>After Tatiana had been released from detention the first time, she was given a special permission to stay in the US-- something called deferred action. Now she was telling me that permission had been revoked.
<v Susan Zalkind>塔蒂亚娜第一次从拘留所被释放后，她获得了留在美国的特别许可——一种叫做“延期行动”（deferred action）的政策。现在她告诉我那个许可被撤销了。

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:09.990
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>Because now they're still overstating that.
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>因为现在他们还在强调那个。

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:13.740
<v Susan Zalkind>Have you been speaking to the FBI since you've been detained?
<v Susan Zalkind>你被拘留后一直在和FBI谈话吗？

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:20.330
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>No, no. Oh my god. Thank god, no. I don't want to talk to them. Maybe I will have more problems now.
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>不，不。天哪。谢天谢地，没有。我不想和他们说话。也许我以后会有更多麻烦。

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:20.810
<v Susan Zalkind>Uh-huh.
<v Susan Zalkind>嗯。

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:22.270
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>I wanted to [INAUDIBLE].
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>我想（听不清）。

00:20:22.270 --> 00:20:22.570
<v Susan Zalkind>OK.
<v Susan Zalkind>好的。

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:35.140
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>I want to be-- I just want to be deported. I just want to see my mom, my family. I just want to go to Russia. But I want to be safe in Russia. I don't want to stay here anymore in the jail.
<v Tatiana Gruzdeva>我想被——我只想被遣返。我只想见到我的妈妈，我的家人。我只想去俄罗斯。但我希望在俄罗斯是安全的。我不想再待在这里的监狱里了。

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:47.140
<v Susan Zalkind>I frantically called immigration officers to find out what was going on. They wouldn't tell me anything. Later, when I asked again, they referred me to the FBI. But the FBI wouldn't talk about it.
<v Susan Zalkind>我疯狂地打电话给移民局官员想弄清楚发生了什么。他们什么也不愿告诉我。后来，当我再次询问时，他们让我去找FBI。但FBI拒绝讨论此事。

00:20:47.140 --> 00:21:03.470
<v Susan Zalkind>After spending 10 days in jail, two immigration agents accompanied Tatiana on her flight back to Moscow. Even when she got home to her mom's house in Moldova, she told me she was too scared to do another interview.
<v Susan Zalkind>在监狱里待了10天后，两名移民局探员陪同塔蒂亚娜登上了飞往莫斯科的航班。即使在她回到摩尔多瓦母亲家中后，她仍告诉我，由于太害怕，她不敢再接受采访。

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:24.350
<v Susan Zalkind>I called around to a few immigration lawyers, because I wanted to know if it's even legal to deport someone for speaking to the press. It turns out, because of Tatiana's status, it was. Under her deferred action, Tatiana was legally allowed to stay in the US for another year. Now that she'd talked to me, her stay was mysteriously cut 10 months short.
<v Susan Zalkind>我打电话咨询了几位移民律师，因为我想知道为了接受媒体采访而遣返某人是否合法。结果发现，由于塔蒂亚娜的身份，这是合法的。根据她的“延期行动”资格，塔蒂亚娜在法律上有权在美国再待一年。现在因为她和我谈话，她的停留时间神秘地缩短了10个月。

00:21:24.350 --> 00:21:38.620
<v Susan Zalkind>But why? What didn't the FBI want her to say to me? Or maybe they were trying to send a message to other people who might talk. I did notice the FBI seemed to be paying a lot of attention to anyone who was close to Ibragim.
<v Susan Zalkind>但是为什么呢？FBI不想让她对我说什么？或者他们是在试图向其他可能开口的人发出信号。我确实注意到FBI似乎对任何与易卜拉欣亲近的人都给予了高度关注。

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:41.640
<v Man>In these recent weeks, how much contact have you had with the FBI?
<v Man>最近几周，你和FBI有多少接触？

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:42.430
<v Khusen Taramov>Me?
<v Khusen Taramov>我吗？

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:53.550
<v Susan Zalkind>This is Ibragim's best friend in Florida, Khusen Taramov. The morning after Ibragim died, he talked to TV reporters with another friend of Ibragim's who didn't want to be identified.
<v Susan Zalkind>这是易卜拉欣在佛罗里达最好的朋友库森·塔拉莫夫（Khusen Taramov）。易卜拉欣去世后的第二天早上，他和易卜拉欣的另一位不想透露姓名的朋友接受了电视记者采访。

00:21:53.550 --> 00:22:00.560
<v Khusen Taramov>They've been following us. They're watching us. Every time we go somewhere, there's two, three cops undercover. We see them.
<v Khusen Taramov>他们一直跟着我们。他们监视着我们。每次我们去什么地方，都有两三个便衣警察。我们看到了他们。

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:07.530
<v Khusen Taramov>You know what I mean? Like, they still watch us all the time. They know everything about us-- where we from, what are we doing.
<v Khusen Taramov>你明白我的意思吗？他们还在一直监视着我们。他们知道关于我们的一切——我们来自哪里，我们在做什么。

00:22:07.530 --> 00:22:08.340
<v Man>They know everything.
<v Man>他们知道一切。

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:12.170
<v Khusen Taramov>Everything. Everything. Everything. So--
<v Khusen Taramov>一切。一切。一切。所以——

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:24.510
<v Susan Zalkind>Khusen had actually been with Ibragim the night he was killed. He went with Ibragim to the final interview at his apartment, but agents told them to wait outside. They wanted to question Ibragim alone.
<v Susan Zalkind>易卜拉欣被杀的那晚，库森实际上和易卜拉欣在一起。他和易卜拉欣一起去了他公寓的那场最终审讯，但探员让他们在外面等。他们想单独审问易卜拉欣。

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:50.890
<v Susan Zalkind>Khusen waited outside for four hours before being told to leave. About an hour later, Ibragim was shot. I wanted to talk to Khusen about this, but when I reached out to him, I learned something strange. Khusen had gone to Russia on July 4th to see his family, and when he tried to come back to the US afterwards, authorities wouldn't let Khusen or his two brothers on the plane, with no explanation.
<v Susan Zalkind>库森在外面等了四个小时才被告知离开。大约一个小时后，易卜拉欣被枪杀。我想和库森谈谈这件事，但当我联系他时，我发现了一件奇怪的事。库森7月4日去俄罗斯看望家人，当他之后尝试回到美国时，当局拒绝让他和他的两个兄弟登机，没有任何解释。

00:22:50.890 --> 00:23:08.260
<v Susan Zalkind>Khusen has a green card. He went to high school in the States. But for some reason, he's not being let back into the country now. When I finally did get a hold of Khusen, he said he didn't want to do an interview. He just wanted to put everything behind him.
<v Susan Zalkind>库森有绿卡。他在美国读过高中。但出于某种原因，他现在无法回到这个国家。当我最终联系上库森时，他说他不想接受采访。他只想把一切抛在脑后。

00:23:08.260 --> 00:23:24.370
<v Susan Zalkind>Here's one more thing. The FBI stopped the medical examiner from releasing Ibragim's autopsy report. The only reason we know how many times he was shot was because pictures were taken of Ibragim's embalmed body. Khusen is the one who took those pictures.
<v Susan Zalkind>还有一件事。FBI阻止法医公布易卜拉欣的尸检报告。我们知道他被开了几枪的唯一原因是因为有人拍了他遗体的照片。库森就是拍下那些照片的人。

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:50.580
<v Susan Zalkind>So here's what I was working with. The FBI killed Ibragim. They released no official information about exactly what happened and wouldn't say when they would release that information. And then these people close to Ibragim were whisked away. His girlfriend, his best friend-- both of them were kept out of the country. I didn't know if it was a cover up, but it just didn't look right.
<v Susan Zalkind>所以，这就是我所面对的情况。FBI杀了易卜拉欣。他们没有发布关于到底发生了什么的官方信息，也不愿说何时会发布信息。然后这些与易卜拉欣亲近的人就被迅速赶走了。他的女朋友、他最好的朋友——他们都被拦在了美国境外。我不知道这是否是一种掩盖，但这看起来实在是不对劲。

00:23:50.580 --> 00:24:09.380
<v Ira Glass>Susan Zalkind. Coming up, a third person close to Ibragim is politely ushered out of the country by federal authorities. And at the end of the hour, our theory about the truth behind all this. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>苏珊·扎尔金德。接下来，第三个与易卜拉欣亲近的人被联邦当局礼貌地赶出了美国。在节目结束时，我们将给出我们关于这一切真相的理论。一分钟后回来，来自芝加哥公共广播和国际公共广播，节目继续。

00:24:09.380 --> 00:24:29.070
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. All this hour, we're hearing Susan Zalkind's investigation into a few deaths-- a triple murder in Waltham, Massachusetts in 2011, where a friend of hers was one of the victims, and a killing last May where the FBI shot a man, Ibragim Todashev, in his own apartment in Florida seven times while questioning him about the triple murder.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。这一整小时，我们都在听苏珊·扎尔金德对几起死亡事件的调查——一起是2011年马萨诸塞州沃尔瑟姆的三尸命案，她的一位朋友是受害者之一；另一起是去年五月的杀人案，易卜拉欣·托达舍夫在佛罗里达的公寓里，在接受关于那起三尸命案的审讯时被FBI开了七枪打死。

00:24:29.070 --> 00:24:50.400
<v Ira Glass>So far, as you have heard, Susan has found that two of the people closest to Todashev, his girlfriend and his best friend, have both been more or less booted out of the United States. And there's a third person who was close to Ibragim whose story is even stranger, Ashur Miraliev. That's where we'll pick up. Here's Susan.
<v Ira Glass>到目前为止，正如你们所听到的，苏珊发现，与托达舍夫最亲近的两个人，他的女朋友和他最好的朋友，基本上都被踢出了美国。还有一个与易卜拉欣亲近的人，他的故事更离奇，阿舒尔·米拉利耶夫（Ashur Miraliev）。我们就从这里继续。下面请听苏珊的讲述。

00:24:50.400 --> 00:25:16.990
<v Susan Zalkind>It took five months to convince Ashur to talk to me. When I first heard about him, he was in a maximum security prison in Florida. Nine weeks later, he was deported to Tajikistan. During all this time, I reached out again and again, and he just kept turning me down. His lawyer said he didn't want to talk because he'd been traumatized, having his friend killed by the FBI and then being arrested himself. But finally, a month ago--
<v Susan Zalkind>我花了五个月才说服阿舒尔和我谈谈。当我第一次听到关于他的消息时，他被关在佛罗里达的一所最高安全级别的监狱里。九周后，他被遣返回塔吉克斯坦。在这期间，我一次又一次地联系他，但他一直拒绝我。他的律师说他不想谈，因为他受到了创伤，经历了朋友被FBI杀害，自己也被捕。但最终，一个月前——

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:18.160
<v Susan Zalkind>Are you there?
<v Susan Zalkind>你在吗？

