<v Ashur Miraliev>They knew that I didn't do nothing. They wasn't interested about me. They just wanted to get some information from me, you know?
<v Ashur Miraliev>他们知道我什么都没做。他们对我没兴趣。他们只是想从我这里得到一些信息，你知道？

00:30:15.910 --> 00:30:31.800
<v Susan Zalkind>I couldn't figure out if the FBI thought Ashur actually knew something or if they were just fishing. It made me think that even if Ibragim confessed, they didn't have much evidence besides it. They needed anything they could find to close the case.
<v Susan Zalkind>我搞不清楚FBI是认为阿舒尔真的知道些什么，还是仅仅在捕风捉影。这让我觉得，即使易卜拉欣招供了，除了他的话，他们也没什么证据。他们需要能找到的一切线索来结案。

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:37.800
<v Susan Zalkind>How long did this interview go for, this first interview?
<v Susan Zalkind>这次审讯持续了多久，第一次审讯？

00:30:37.800 --> 00:31:16.490
<v Ashur Miraliev>Um, it was like a few hours. A few hours, yeah. And when I told them I wanted to stop the conversation, they told me you're gonna go to the jail, you know? That's the first time they told me about the jail. I really thought they were kidding, you know? Because I knew that I wouldn't go to the jail for my driver's license, because it was only nine days expired.
<v Ashur Miraliev>嗯，持续了几个小时。几个小时吧。当我告诉他们我想停止对话时，他们告诉我你会进监狱的，你知道？那是他们第一次提到监狱。我真的以为他们在开玩笑，你知道？因为我知道我就因为驾照过期不会坐牢，因为那才过期九天。

00:31:16.490 --> 00:31:28.860
<v Ashur Miraliev>I could get only a ticket, you know? I thought they were kidding, but they weren't. They just put handcuffs on me. They took me to the jail. I went to the jail, you know?
<v Ashur Miraliev>我本可以只收到一张罚单，你知道？我以为他们在开玩笑，但他们不是。他们直接给我戴上手铐。他们把我带到监狱。我进了监狱，你知道？

00:31:28.860 --> 00:31:51.380
<v Susan Zalkind>Ashur had no idea what he was being arrested for, and when they told him, he couldn't believe it. He thought there was some mistake. I just want to stop here to take a detour in Ashur's story in order to explain his arrest charge. It sheds light on how the FBI was operating down in Florida in this case, and the lengths they were willing to go to get to Ashur.
<v Susan Zalkind>阿舒尔根本不知道自己是因为什么被捕的，当他们告诉他时，他简直不敢相信。他以为是个误会。我想在这里停一下，绕个弯说明一下阿舒尔的指控。这揭示了FBI在此案中在佛罗里达的操作方式，以及他们为了抓到阿舒尔愿意采取的手段。

00:31:51.380 --> 00:32:33.610
<v Susan Zalkind>Ashur was arrested for threatening the victim of a crime. The formal charge was tampering with a victim by threat, and the story behind it starts here, at the Ali Baba Hookah Lounge just outside Orlando. Ali Baba sits in the front of a small shopping plaza on Polynesian Isle Boulevard. Disney World is down the road, but the plaza is like this international oasis in the middle of American tourist country. There's a small grocery store owned by Egyptians, a hair salon owned by an Iraqi Turkish guy, and the hookah bar, which is owned by a Moroccan.
<v Susan Zalkind>阿舒尔因威胁犯罪受害者而被捕。正式罪名是“通过威胁干扰受害者”（tampering with a victim by threat），而这背后的故事始于这里，奥兰多郊外的阿里·巴巴水烟吧。阿里·巴巴坐落在波利尼西亚岛大道旁一个小购物广场的前面。迪士尼世界就在那条路尽头，但这个广场就像美国旅游区中间的一个国际绿洲。有一家埃及人拥有的小杂货店，一家伊拉克裔土耳其人拥有的美发沙龙，以及那家由摩洛哥人拥有的水烟吧。

00:32:33.610 --> 00:32:42.030
<v Susan Zalkind>The incident that put Ashur in jail happened here, and the person who pressed charges against Ashur was the manager of the hookah bar, this guy.
<v Susan Zalkind>让阿舒尔进监狱的事件就发生在这里，而指控阿舒尔的人是水烟吧的经理，就是这家伙。

00:32:42.030 --> 00:32:47.170
<v Youness Eit Dammou>My name is Youness eit Dammou. I used to work at the Ali Baba Hookah Bar.
<v Youness Eit Dammou>我叫尤尼斯·艾特·达穆（Youness eit Dammou）。我以前在阿里·巴巴水烟吧工作。

00:32:47.170 --> 00:33:12.010
<v Susan Zalkind>Youness actually works at a different hookah bar now, across the street. When my producer, Brian Reed, and I found Youness, it was midnight. He graciously sat down and told us the story of how he came to press charges against Ashur. It actually begins with Ibragim. One day, Youness says, Ibragim was at the hookah bar, and he was hitting on a woman who worked there, making her feel uncomfortable, so Youness went over to intervene.
<v Susan Zalkind>尤尼斯现在在街对面另一家水烟吧工作。当我和制片人布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）找到尤尼斯时，已经是午夜了。他很慷慨地坐下来，告诉了我们他是如何决定指控阿舒尔的故事。这其实始于易卜拉欣。尤尼斯说，有一天易卜拉欣在水烟吧，搭讪了一名在那里工作的女性，让她感到不舒服，于是尤尼斯过去干预。

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:35.740
<v Youness Eit Dammou>Ibragim, he got a little bit frustrated and, you know, he thinks he can manage the place. I told him, honestly, I'm the manager over here. You're welcome. You can have a good time, you know, enjoy yourself. But if you want to rule this place as you want, you're welcome to leave. So obviously he didn't like what I said.
<v Youness Eit Dammou>易卜拉欣变得有点沮丧，你知道，他以为他能管这个地方。我告诉他，说实话，我是这里的经理。欢迎光临。你可以玩得开心，你知道，享受你自己。但如果你想随心所欲地统治这里，那么欢迎你离开。显然他不喜欢我说的话。

00:33:35.740 --> 00:33:48.280
<v Susan Zalkind>After this, Youness says he walked over to the grocery store in the plaza. Ibragim followed him, telling Youness he wanted to go out back with him behind the plaza. Youness turned away, and then, all of a sudden--
<v Susan Zalkind>在那之后，尤尼斯说他走到广场里的杂货店。易卜拉欣跟着他，告诉尤尼斯他想去广场后面跟他聊聊。尤尼斯转身离开，然后，突然间——

00:33:48.280 --> 00:33:51.280
<v Youness Eit Dammou>Boom! I lost consciousness.
<v Youness Eit Dammou>砰！我失去了知觉。

00:33:51.280 --> 00:33:56.740
<v Susan Zalkind>Ibragim knocked Youness on the side of the head with his fist. A few seconds later, when Youness came to--
<v Susan Zalkind>易卜拉欣用拳头猛击了尤尼斯的头部侧面。几秒钟后，当尤尼斯醒来——

00:33:56.740 --> 00:33:59.680
<v Youness Eit Dammou>He grabbed me from my legs, like this.
<v Youness Eit Dammou>他抓住了我的腿，就像这样。

00:33:59.680 --> 00:34:18.820
<v Susan Zalkind>Youness says Ibragim lifted him high off the ground until Youness's knees were level with Ibragim's chest. Then Ibragim slammed him onto the sidewalk, like in a wrestling move. Remember, he was training to be a professional fighter. Ibragim climbed on top of him. At this point, a crowd had gathered, and Youness yelled for someone to call 911.
<v Susan Zalkind>尤尼斯说易卜拉欣把他从地上高高举起，直到尤尼斯的膝盖与易卜拉欣的胸部齐平。然后易卜拉欣把他猛摔在人行道上，就像一个摔跤动作。记住，他曾训练成为一名职业格斗选手。易卜拉欣骑在他身上。此时，人群聚集了起来，尤尼斯大喊让人拨打911。

00:34:18.820 --> 00:34:31.510
<v Youness Eit Dammou>He heard me say, call 911. He ran to his car and ran.
<v Youness Eit Dammou>他听到我说拨打911。他跑向车子逃走了。

00:34:31.510 --> 00:34:47.650
<v Susan Zalkind>When police came, Youness told him everything he knew about Ibragim, which wasn't much. He didn't even remember his last name. After police left, Youness was trying to get more information about the guy who beat him up, so he went to a pizza shop next door where he'd seen Ibragim hanging out a lot.
<v Susan Zalkind>当警察来时，尤尼斯告诉了他们关于易卜拉欣所知的一切，其实并不多。他甚至记不得他的姓。警察走后，尤尼斯试图寻找更多关于那个打他的人的信息，于是他去了隔壁一家易卜拉欣经常在那闲逛的披萨店。

00:34:47.650 --> 00:34:55.950
<v Susan Zalkind>That's when he ran into Ashur. Ashur was a delivery guy there, and Youness says Ashur didn't like the fact that he was asking about Ibragim.
<v Susan Zalkind>就在那时他遇到了阿舒尔。阿舒尔是那里的外卖员，尤尼斯说阿舒尔不喜欢他打听易卜拉欣的事。

00:34:55.950 --> 00:35:08.170
<v Youness Eit Dammou>When I asked, Ashur got pissed, and he told me, you're not a man. Why you call the cops? Why you didn't fight him like a man? He starts cursing, and he was coming toward me.
<v Youness Eit Dammou>当我打听时，阿舒尔很生气，他告诉我，你不是个男人。你为什么要报警？你为什么不像个男人一样和他打架？他开始咒骂，并朝我走来。

00:35:08.170 --> 00:35:09.290
<v Susan Zalkind>Did he threaten you?
<v Susan Zalkind>他威胁你了吗？

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:10.520
<v Youness Eit Dammou>Yeah.
<v Youness Eit Dammou>是的。

00:35:10.520 --> 00:35:11.900
<v Susan Zalkind>What did he say?
<v Susan Zalkind>他说了什么？

00:35:11.900 --> 00:35:17.840
<v Youness Eit Dammou>I don't remember exactly, but it's like he wanted to provoke me to get into a fight.
<v Youness Eit Dammou>我不记得确切的字句，但就像他想挑衅我，让我去打架。

00:35:17.840 --> 00:35:19.850
<v Brian Reed>And did you consider calling the police?
<v Brian Reed>那你考虑过报警吗？

00:35:19.850 --> 00:35:32.080
<v Youness Eit Dammou>No, I didn't, because I'd just called him like an hour and a half ago, something like that. There is better things for the police to do than a fight between me and someone, you know what I mean?
<v Youness Eit Dammou>没有，我没有，因为我一个半小时前刚打过电话，差不多是这样。警察有比处理我和某人打架更好的事情要做，你明白我的意思吗？

00:35:32.080 --> 00:35:44.780
<v Susan Zalkind>This incident, this little shouting match, this is what Ashur ended up being arrested for. Because Ashur yelled at Youness for calling the police on Ibragim. This is the tampering with a victim charge.
<v Susan Zalkind>这次事件，这场小争吵，就是阿舒尔最终被捕的原因。因为阿舒尔大声斥责尤尼斯报警抓易卜拉欣。这就是那项“干扰受害者”的指控。

00:35:44.780 --> 00:36:02.380
<v Susan Zalkind>What makes this especially strange is that Ashur wasn't arrested until more than a year later, even though he worked right next door to Youness. That's because in all that time, Youness never thought this was a big enough deal to even call the police about. Youness's only beef was with Ibragim for beating him up.
<v Susan Zalkind>特别奇怪的是，阿舒尔直到一年多后才被捕，尽管他在尤尼斯隔壁工作。那是因为在这段时间里，尤尼斯从未觉得这事大到需要报警。尤尼斯唯一的怨气是针对易卜拉欣打了他。

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:24.100
<v Susan Zalkind>He did want to press charges against him, but 10 months went by, and there was never an arrest. Then the Boston Marathon is bombed, and Youness says one day, out of the blue, an FBI agent met with him, along with a detective from Osceola County, who asked if he still wanted to press charges against Ibragim.
<v Susan Zalkind>他确实想指控易卜拉欣，但10个月过去了，一直没有逮捕行动。然后波士顿马拉松被炸了，尤尼斯说有一天，毫无预兆地，一名FBI探员带着一名奥西奥拉县（Osceola County）的侦探找上门，问他是否还想指控易卜拉欣。

00:36:24.100 --> 00:36:27.190
<v Susan Zalkind>Did they mention the Boston Marathon bombing?
<v Susan Zalkind>他们提到波士顿马拉松爆炸案了吗？

00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:41.700
<v Youness Eit Dammou>No. No. We didn't discuss that. The things that we discussed is the fights, and they want me to press charges. And I press charges.
<v Youness Eit Dammou>没有。没有。我们没有讨论那个。我们讨论的是那次打架，他们想要我指控他。我就指控了。

00:36:41.700 --> 00:36:58.700
<v Susan Zalkind>Now, if this were a normal case, police would take those charges and go arrest Ibragim. But for some reason, he wasn't arrested. Just four days later, Ibragim was dead.
<v Susan Zalkind>现在，如果是普通的案子，警察会拿着这些指控去抓易卜拉欣。但出于某种原因，他没被抓。仅仅四天后，易卜拉欣就死了。

00:36:58.700 --> 00:37:11.770
<v Susan Zalkind>Three months went by, and Youness got another visit, again from the Osceola County detective. This time, he asked if Youness wanted to press charges against Ashur. And Youness said OK.
<v Susan Zalkind>三个月过去了，尤尼斯又接到了拜访，还是那位奥西奥拉县的侦探。这次，他问尤尼斯是否想指控阿舒尔。尤尼斯说可以。

00:37:11.770 --> 00:37:23.010
<v Susan Zalkind>About three weeks later, Ashur was pulled over by the undercover cars and taken for the FBI interview. Which brings us back to the moment FBI agents told him he was under arrest.
<v Susan Zalkind>大约三周后，阿舒尔被便衣警车拦下，并被带去参加FBI的审讯。这就让我们回到了FBI探员告诉他被捕的那一刻。

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:31.170
<v Ashur Miraliev>I asked them, why am I here? Why am I in a jail? What are the charges, you know?
<v Ashur Miraliev>我问他们，我为什么在这里？我为什么在监狱里？指控是什么，你知道？

00:37:31.170 --> 00:37:33.230
<v Susan Zalkind>Again, Ashur Miraliev.
<v Susan Zalkind>再次回到阿舒尔·米拉利耶夫。

00:37:33.230 --> 00:37:46.000
<v Ashur Miraliev>And they said, do you remember the incident with Youness over there in a hookah bar? I said, yes, so what? They said, that's why you're here.
<v Ashur Miraliev>他们说，你记得水烟吧发生的那次事件吗？我说，记得，那又怎样？他们说，这就是你在这里的原因。

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:53.400
<v Susan Zalkind>Ashur thought there must be some mistake, so much so that as soon as he got booked into jail, he said he wanted to talk to the FBI again.
<v Susan Zalkind>阿舒尔认为一定是个误会，以至于他一进监狱就说他想再次和FBI谈话。

00:37:53.400 --> 00:38:10.260
<v Ashur Miraliev>He drove me back to the agents. I told them, hey, guys, it's impossible, you know? Are you guys kidding or something? I just couldn't believe. They told me that they have nothing to do with that.
<v Ashur Miraliev>他把我带回探员那里。我告诉他们，嘿，伙计们，这不可能，你知道？你们是在开玩笑吗？我简直不敢相信。他们告诉我，他们和那个毫无关系。

00:38:10.260 --> 00:38:22.670
<v Ashur Miraliev>It's the state. State has some charges on me, you know? That's all what they told me, that they don't know nothing about my charges. They don't know nothing.
<v Ashur Miraliev>这是州里的事。州对我有一些指控，你知道？他们就告诉我这些，他们不知道关于我的指控的事。他们什么都不知道。

00:38:22.670 --> 00:38:46.050
<v Susan Zalkind>It's not true that the FBI knew nothing about this charge. A spokeswoman from the Osceola County Sheriff's Office confirmed that an FBI agent named Sykes was there for some of Youness's questioning. And Ashur's arrest affidavit said Agent Sykes is the one who gave them Ashur's name.
<v Susan Zalkind>FBI什么都不知道是不真实的。奥西奥拉县治安官办公室的一位发言人证实，一名叫赛克斯（Sykes）的FBI探员确实在场参与了尤尼斯的部分审讯。而阿舒尔的逮捕证词上写着，是赛克斯探员给了他们阿舒尔的名字。

00:38:46.050 --> 00:39:03.490
<v Susan Zalkind>That wasn't all, though. On his booking papers, along with the tampering with a victim charge, Ashur was given this designation with no explanation-- "on terrorist watch list," "police protective custody," and "high risk, house alone."
<v Susan Zalkind>那还不是全部。在他的登记文件上，除了“干扰受害者”的指控外，阿舒尔还被赋予了这个标签，没有任何解释——“恐怖分子观察名单”、“警方保护性羁押”、“高风险，独自居住”。

00:39:03.490 --> 00:39:23.470
<v Susan Zalkind>I should maybe be explicit here. Never once was it alleged, either in a formal charge or even in informal leaks, that Ashur had anything to do with the Boston bombings. The most you can say is how Ashur himself puts it. He knew a guy who knew a guy who supposedly committed the marathon bombings.
<v Susan Zalkind>我在这里可能需要明确一下。无论是在正式指控中还是非正式泄密中，从未有人声称阿舒尔与波士顿爆炸案有任何关系。最多你只能说出阿舒尔本人的说法：他认识一个人，那个人认识据称犯下马拉松爆炸案的人。

00:39:23.470 --> 00:39:41.320
<v Susan Zalkind>For the crime of yelling at Youness, Ashur spent 33 days in a maximum security cell-- an 8' by 10' room where he spent all day alone. There were no windows. Just a slit in the door looking out to the lobby. The lights were always on, and he was always cold.
<v Susan Zalkind>因为对尤尼斯大喊大叫的罪行，阿舒尔在最高安全级别的牢房里待了33天——一个8英尺乘10英尺的房间，他整天独自待在那里。没有窗户。门上只有一个狭缝，可以看到走廊。灯总是亮着，而且他一直很冷。

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:57.250
<v Susan Zalkind>I talked to his attorney, a woman named Thania Diaz Clevenger, who, after trying for several days, and experiencing what she said was the most trouble she's ever had trying to locate a client, was finally able to meet with Ashur. This is how she described him in jail.
<v Susan Zalkind>我和他的律师谈过，一个名叫塔尼亚·迪亚兹·克莱文格（Thania Diaz Clevenger）的女士。在尝试了好几天并经历了她所说的在寻找客户方面遇到过的最大麻烦后，她终于见到了阿舒尔。她这样描述在监狱里的他。

00:39:57.250 --> 00:40:33.100
<v Thania Diaz Clevenger>He was broken. He was petrified and broken and crying. And they chained him, arms, legs. He had the belly chain. Two guards walked with us down the hall to another room where they chained him to the ground, and he begged me not to go.
<v Thania Diaz Clevenger>他崩溃了。他被吓坏了，崩溃了，在哭。他们锁着他，胳膊、腿。他戴着腹部锁链。两名警卫陪我们沿着走廊走到另一个房间，在那里他们把他锁在地板上，他乞求我不要走。

00:40:33.100 --> 00:40:47.850
<v Susan Zalkind>The charge that got Ashur thrown in prison, tampering with the victim of a crime, was dropped after a month. The state attorney's office wrote, quote, "This case is not suitable for prosecution." But for Ashur, it was too late.
<v Susan Zalkind>导致阿舒尔被关进监狱的指控——干扰受害者——在一个月后被撤销。州检察官办公室写道：“此案不适合起诉。”但对阿舒尔来说，已经太晚了。

00:40:47.850 --> 00:41:21.790
<v Susan Zalkind>When he was arrested, his visa had already expired. He was in the process of applying for asylum, but he missed a court date while he was in jail. So when he was moved out of maximum security, they transferred him to an immigration jail. Nine weeks after the Orlando PD pulled him over for the traffic stop, he was deported back to Tajikistan, leaving behind his apartment, all of his belongings, his clothes, his money, and his car.
<v Susan Zalkind>他被捕时，签证已经过期。他正在申请庇护，但因为在监狱里错过了法庭日期。所以当他从最高安全级别牢房转移出来时，他们把他转到了移民监狱。在奥兰多警方因交通拦截拦下他九周后，他被遣返回塔吉克斯坦，留下了他的公寓、所有的随身物品、衣服、钱和汽车。

00:41:21.790 --> 00:41:50.550
<v Susan Zalkind>Ashur's story seems so extreme. For a guy who just knew a guy who knew a guy, the government took pretty drastic measures against him. And when you add what they did to Ashur to the fact that Tatiana and Khusen are also being kept out of the country, it's hard not to ask, why is the FBI doing this? Are they cleaning up their mess? Trying to hide something? I asked a former FBI agent named Don Borelli what he thought.
<v Susan Zalkind>阿舒尔的故事看起来太极端了。对于一个只是认识一个认识那个人的人，政府对他采取了相当严厉的措施。当你把他经历的事与塔蒂亚娜和库森也被拦在国外的事实加在一起时，很难不去问，FBI为什么要这么做？他们是在清理烂摊子吗？试图掩盖什么吗？我问了一位名叫唐·博雷利（Don Borelli）的前FBI探员他的看法。

00:41:50.550 --> 00:42:04.240
<v Don Borelli>If you think that this is some conspiracy to get these people out of the limelight where you're basically trying to suppress a story or a cover up or anything, I don't believe that that's the case at all.
<v Don Borelli>如果你认为这是一场阴谋，为了把这些人排除在公众视野之外，你本质上是在试图抑制一个报道或掩盖什么，我不相信情况是那样。

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:37.670
<v Susan Zalkind>What happened to Ashur, Tatiana, and Khusen doesn't make a lot of sense for a murder case, but it does for a terrorism case, which, of course, this was. Don worked at the FBI for 25 years and did a lot of terrorism cases. He doesn't have inside knowledge of the investigation into Ibragim, but I told him about the tactics used against Ibragim's friends-- deportations, no fly lists, arresting someone on local charges. He says these are all standard in a terrorism investigation for anyone who has any connections to the case.
<v Susan Zalkind>发生在阿舒尔、塔蒂亚娜和库森身上的事对于谋杀案来说讲不通，但对于恐怖主义案件就讲得通了，当然，这起案子确实是。唐在FBI工作了25年，处理过很多恐怖主义案件。他没有关于易卜拉欣调查的内幕消息，但我告诉了他针对易卜拉欣朋友所采取的策略——遣返、禁飞名单、以当地指控逮捕某人。他说这些都是恐怖主义调查中针对任何与案件有联系的人的标准操作。

00:42:37.670 --> 00:43:10.000
<v Don Borelli>It may be third or four persons removed, but that's the nature of a terrorism investigation. You cast a very wide net. So yeah, if you can keep people out of the country that you think could potentially do harm or aid others in doing harm or whatever, you know, even provide financing, for example, then you are obligated to do that. That's your job to do that.
<v Don Borelli>可能隔了三四个人，但这就是恐怖主义调查的本质。你撒了一张非常大的网。所以是的，如果你能让那些你认为可能潜在造成伤害或协助他人造成伤害的人——哪怕是提供资金——离开这个国家，那你就有义务这么做。那是你的工作。

00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:32.970
<v Susan Zalkind>Don says that even if it doesn't seem like you're going to do any harm, if you can be deported, standard procedure in terrorism cases is to deport you unless you can offer something to federal agents that makes it worth it to keep you around. In other words, there could be a logical explanation for keeping Ashur, Tatiana, and Khusen out of the country that doesn't involve a cover up.
<v Susan Zalkind>唐说，即使看起来你好像不会造成任何伤害，如果可以遣返，恐怖主义案件中的标准程序就是遣返你，除非你能向联邦探员提供一些值得保留你的价值的东西。换句话说，保留阿舒尔、塔蒂亚娜和库森在国外可能有一个合乎逻辑的解释，并不涉及掩盖真相。

00:43:32.970 --> 00:44:01.850
<v Susan Zalkind>But that still doesn't explain the many weird things going on in this case. It doesn't explain why the FBI has taken so long-- nearly 10 months-- to release the report on Ibragim's shooting. It doesn't tell us what to make of Ibragim's supposed confession or what brought agents to his living room in the first place, whether they have any solid proof at all linking Ibragim or Tamerlan Tsarnaev to the murders.
<v Susan Zalkind>但这仍然不能解释此案中发生的许多奇怪的事情。它不能解释为什么FBI花了这么长时间——近10个月——来发布关于易卜拉欣枪击事件的报告。它也没有告诉我们该如何理解易卜拉欣所谓的供词，或者是什么把探员带到了他的客厅，他们是否真的有任何确凿的证据把易卜拉欣或塔梅尔兰·萨纳耶夫与谋杀案联系起来。

00:44:01.850 --> 00:44:41.170
<v Susan Zalkind>In the course of reporting this story, I've reached out to the Waltham Police Department, the Middlesex District Attorney's Office, the Massachusetts State Police, the Massachusetts Attorney General, the State Attorney for Osceola and Orange Counties, the Orlando Police Department, the Department of Justice, and of course, the FBI, and none of them will comment. But I've also talked to dozens of people connected to the murders and connected to Ibragim and the Tsarnaevs, as well as to attorneys in law enforcement. And I do have a theory about what happened in Ibragim's apartment and in the months before and after. It's just a theory, but here goes.
<v Susan Zalkind>在报道这个故事的过程中，我联系了沃尔瑟姆警察局、米德尔塞克斯地方检察官办公室、马萨诸塞州警察局、马萨诸塞州总检察长、奥西奥拉和奥兰治县州检察官、奥兰多警察局、司法部，当然还有FBI，他们都不愿置评。但我还和几十个与谋杀案有关的人、与易卜拉欣和萨纳耶夫兄弟有关的人，以及执法部门的律师谈过。我确实有一个关于易卜拉欣公寓里发生的事情以及前后的几个月里发生了什么的理论。这只是个理论，但且听我说。

00:44:41.170 --> 00:45:05.160
<v Susan Zalkind>As best as I can tell, the FBI showed up at Ibragim's door looking for a terrorist. He'd spoken on the phone with Tamerlan Tsarnaev in the month before the bombing, and I know a guy named John Allan who tipped off investigators. John's the owner of a mixed martial arts gym in Boston where both Tamerlan Tsarnaev and Ibragim trained. He says he was on a trip in Thailand when the bombings happened.
<v Susan Zalkind>据我所知，FBI找上易卜拉欣的门是想找恐怖分子。他在爆炸案发生前一个月曾与塔梅尔兰·萨纳耶夫通电话，我知道一个叫约翰·艾伦（John Allan）的人向调查人员通风报信。约翰是波士顿一家综合格斗健身房的老板，塔梅尔兰·萨纳耶夫和易卜拉欣都在那儿训练。他说爆炸案发生时他正在泰国旅行。

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:17.990
<v John Allan>Before I even got back, you know, I had a Facebook message. One of my cop students-- one of my students that's a policeman-- that has Ibragim's information. Give it to him. You should probably check this guy out.
<v John Allan>甚至在我回来之前，你知道，我就收到了Facebook信息。我的一名警察学生——我的一名当警察的学生——有易卜拉欣的信息。把它给他。你应该检查下这个人。

00:45:17.990 --> 00:45:26.810
<v Susan Zalkind>John says that several times, he saw Tamerlan and Ibragim praying at the gym, which he thought was odd. Plus, he says, Ibragim just seemed kind of crazy.
<v Susan Zalkind>约翰说，有几次他看到塔梅尔兰和易卜拉欣在健身房祷告，他觉得这很奇怪。另外，他说，易卜拉欣看起来有点疯狂。

00:45:26.810 --> 00:45:43.000
<v John Allan>I mean, he was definitely out there. He was definitely very Muslim. And if you were to ask me at the time if someone was capable of doing that, I would have said Ibragim not Tamerlan, you know? And I'm talking about the bombing.
<v John Allan>我的意思是，他确实非常激进。他确实非常穆斯林。如果你当时问我是否有人有能力做那种事，我会说是易卜拉欣而不是塔梅尔兰，你知道？我说的是爆炸案。

00:45:43.000 --> 00:45:56.440
<v Susan Zalkind>Within a few days of this tip, FBI agents were at Ibragim's apartment. John says it never occurred to him that Ibragim could have been involved in the Waltham triple murders. He didn't mention anything like that to the police or FBI.
<v Susan Zalkind>收到此线索几天内，FBI探员就在易卜拉欣的公寓里了。约翰说他从没想过易卜拉欣可能卷入了沃尔瑟姆三尸命案。他没向警察或FBI提到过任何类似的事。

00:45:56.440 --> 00:46:14.670
<v Susan Zalkind>But by the time they locked up Tatiana, about two and a half weeks later, they'd made the connection. Where that suspicion came from exactly, that Ibragim was involved in the Waltham murders, I just don't know. I ran down every path I could think of to try to find something linking Ibragim to the killings.
<v Susan Zalkind>但在大约两周半后他们拘留塔蒂亚娜时，他们已经建立了联系。那种怀疑，即易卜拉欣卷入了沃尔瑟姆谋杀案，究竟从何而来，我实在不知道。为了找到连接易卜拉欣与命案的任何线索，我尝试了我想到的每一条路径。

00:46:14.670 --> 00:46:46.870
<v Susan Zalkind>I looked into some established crime rings in Waltham, but couldn't connect them to Ibragim. I tried finding evidence that he was even in the Boston area in September of 2011 when the murders happened. I pulled his driving records, talked to landlords, employers, roommates. I can place him in Boston a month before the murders and a few months after, but not in September. His wife claims he was in Atlanta with her at the time, but she has no proof.
<v Susan Zalkind>我调查了沃尔瑟姆的一些既有犯罪团伙，但无法将他们与易卜拉欣联系起来。我尝试寻找他在2011年9月谋杀案发生时甚至身在波士顿地区的证据。我查了他的驾驶记录、咨询了房东、雇主、室友。我可以确认他在谋杀案发生前一个月和后几个月在波士顿，但9月份不行。他妻子声称他当时和她在亚特兰大，但她没有任何证据。

00:46:46.870 --> 00:47:09.360
<v Susan Zalkind>Not long after Ibragim was killed, law enforcement sources told The New York Times that they put together, quote, "a lot of circumstantial pieces" that they then used to pressure Ibragim into implicating himself and Tamerlan in the Waltham murders. It's possible that that's all they had, circumstantial pieces, which wouldn't go very far at trial.
<v Susan Zalkind>易卜拉欣被杀后不久，执法部门消息人士告诉《纽约时报》，他们搜集了“大量间接证据”，然后用这些证据向易卜拉欣施压，迫使他指认自己和塔梅尔兰卷入了沃尔瑟姆谋杀案。有可能他们只有这些，间接证据，这在法庭审判中走不了多远。

00:47:09.360 --> 00:47:36.100
<v Susan Zalkind>So again, this is my theory. But because their evidence was thin, I think law enforcement and prosecutors needed a confession. They needed to put pressure on Ibragim to get him to crack. And in fact, a law enforcement official told The New York Times that to get Ibragim to talk about the triple murders, investigators, quote, "made him believe he's done, and the only way he could help himself is confess and lay out the details of what had happened."
<v Susan Zalkind>所以再次声明，这是我的理论。但因为他们的证据薄弱，我认为执法部门和检察官需要一份供词。他们需要向易卜拉欣施压，让他崩溃。事实上，一名执法官员告诉《纽约时报》，为了让易卜拉欣谈论三尸命案，调查人员“让他相信他完了，唯一能帮助自己的办法就是供认并说出发生细节。”

00:47:36.100 --> 00:47:44.700
<v Susan Zalkind>But then the officials said that Ibragim flipped out. Which brings up the question, was the FBI shooting of Ibragim justified?
<v Susan Zalkind>但随后官员们说易卜拉欣发狂了。这就引出了一个问题：FBI枪杀易卜拉欣合理吗？

00:47:44.700 --> 00:47:53.650
<v John Allan>If Ibragim became irate and aggressive and attacked the guy, I can understand why he unloaded his weapon. Ibragim wasn't going to stop.
<v John Allan>如果易卜拉欣变得愤怒、充满攻击性并袭击了那个人，我可以理解他为什么清空弹夹。易卜拉欣是不会停下的。

00:47:53.650 --> 00:48:01.360
<v Susan Zalkind>This is John Allan again, the owner of the MMA gym Ibragim and Tamerlan went to. John trained Ibragim for his professional fights.
<v Susan Zalkind>这是约翰·艾伦，易卜拉欣和塔梅尔兰去过的综合格斗健身房老板。约翰曾训练易卜拉欣参加职业比赛。

00:48:01.360 --> 00:48:10.440
<v John Allan>I mean, unless there was a mortal wound that ended his life right there, trust me, I mean, he would break every bone in his body to headbutt somebody.
<v John Allan>我的意思是，除非当时有致命伤让他当场结束生命，相信我，我的意思是，他会折断自己身上的每一根骨头去头槌某人。

00:48:10.440 --> 00:48:34.040
<v Susan Zalkind>John Allan, of course, wasn't in the room when Ibragim was shot. But he says Ibragim was a very good fighter and had a short fuse, and there were other times Ibragim snapped and got violent. There was the incident at the hookah bar where he beat up Youness, of course. But Ibragim also got arrested for beating a guy unconscious over a parking space at a mall in Florida, and for a road rage incident in Boston.
<v Susan Zalkind>约翰·艾伦当然在易卜拉欣被枪杀时不在房间里。但他表示，易卜拉欣是一名非常优秀的格斗选手，性子急躁，还有其他时候易卜拉欣也曾失控并变得暴力。当然，有水烟吧那起打伤尤尼斯的事件。但易卜拉欣还曾因在佛罗里达一家商场的停车位上把一个人打昏而被捕，在波士顿也发生过路怒事件。

00:48:34.040 --> 00:48:40.450
<v Susan Zalkind>Certain stuff just set him off. John Allan remembers he had a weird thing about one particular swear word.
<v Susan Zalkind>有些事情就是会让他爆发。约翰·艾伦记得他对一个特定的脏话很敏感。

00:48:40.450 --> 00:48:58.770
<v John Allan>You know, if somebody were to say mother expletive to him, he would lose it. I mean, he would lose it. He'd be ready to fight 17 people and not care if he would win or lose, or he would just lose it. Sometimes it wouldn't even be directed at him.
<v John Allan>你知道，如果有人对他说那种辱骂母亲的脏话，他会失去理智。我的意思是，他会失去理智。他会准备好跟17个人干架，根本不在乎输赢，或者他就是会失去理智。有时那甚至不是针对他的。

00:48:58.770 --> 00:49:15.040
<v John Allan>I tried to explain to him, like, listen, it's not a literal term in America. It's just a slang saying. It doesn't mean literally, you know? But there was definitely something going on with him and anger issues.
<v John Allan>我试着向他解释，听着，这在美国不是个字面术语。这只是俚语。这不代表字面意义，你知道？但他确实有某些易怒问题。

00:49:15.040 --> 00:49:33.680
<v Susan Zalkind>According to the police report for Ibragim's road rage arrest, Ibragim was heard yelling at another driver, quote, "you say something about my mother, I will kill you."
<v Susan Zalkind>根据易卜拉欣路怒被捕的警察报告，有人听到易卜拉欣对着另一名司机大喊：“你再说我母亲，我就杀了你。”

00:49:33.680 --> 00:50:00.220
<v Susan Zalkind>Since the FBI report hasn't come out explaining what happened that night in Ibragim's apartment, I turn to David Boeri. He's a crime and courts reporter for Boston Public Radio Station WBUR. He's covered the FBI for 30 years and has reported on the night Ibragim Todashev was shot. I wanted to know what his law enforcement sources told him about that night. David says he talked to several law enforcement sources who heard or read accounts from the people in the room.
<v Susan Zalkind>由于FBI报告尚未解释那晚在易卜拉欣公寓里发生了什么，我转向了大卫·博伊里（David Boeri）。他是波士顿公共广播电台WBUR的法庭记者。他报道FBI已经30年了，并报道了易卜拉欣·托达舍夫被枪杀的那晚。我想知道他的执法部门消息人士告诉他关于那个晚上的什么。大卫说他与几位执法部门消息人士谈过，他们听过或读过房间里的人的描述。

00:50:00.220 --> 00:50:10.090
<v David Boeri>Inside his apartment-- it's a very small apartment. You've been there. You saw how small it was. There are three people from Boston.
<v David Boeri>在他的公寓里——这是一个非常小的公寓。你去过那里。你看到了它有多小。房间里有三个人，来自波士顿。

00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:33.460
<v Susan Zalkind>The three people were an FBI agent who'd come down from Boston and two Massachusetts state troopers. I have been in that apartment when I visited Tatiana. It's one square room with wall to wall carpeting and a lofted bed. It sits by the side of a swamp. The entire back side of the house is made of shuttered glass windows. There's air conditioning, but in the muggy Orlando weather, it's not enough.
<v Susan Zalkind>那三个人是一名从波士顿下来的FBI探员和两名马萨诸塞州警。我拜访塔蒂亚娜时曾去过那个公寓。它是一个带有墙到墙地毯和高架床的方形房间。它坐落在沼泽边上。房子的整个背面由百叶窗玻璃窗组成。有空调，但在奥兰多闷热的天气里，这根本不够。

00:50:33.460 --> 00:50:43.960
<v David Boeri>Well, we also know from one of the people in that room, according to accounts of what he said, it was hot. It was very hot. He was sweating that night.
<v David Boeri>嗯，我们也从房间里的一个人那里知道，根据他所说内容的陈述，当时很热。非常热。那天晚上他在出汗。

00:50:43.960 --> 00:50:50.230
<v Susan Zalkind>David says they began questioning Ibragim about the Waltham murders. The interview started at 7:30.
<v Susan Zalkind>大卫说他们开始审问易卜拉欣关于沃尔瑟姆谋杀案的事。审讯在7点30分开始。

00:50:50.230 --> 00:51:02.700
<v David Boeri>In those rooms, if you've ever seen tapes of interrogations taking place-- and I have-- they are fraught with tension, accusation. They're pushing him. That's what interrogators do.
<v David Boeri>在那些房间里，如果你看过审讯录像——我看过——它们充满了紧张和指责。他们在逼迫他。这就是审讯人员所做的。

00:51:02.700 --> 00:51:22.110
<v David Boeri>Push him. No, I don't believe that. No, I don't believe that. You're telling me this, that, whatever. It's churning up conflict and stress. And they are trained to deal in stress when they're doing interrogations to break down their suspect.
<v David Boeri>逼迫他。不，我不信。不，我不信。你告诉我的这个，那个，随便什么。它在制造冲突和压力。他们在审讯时经过训练要利用压力来瓦解嫌疑人。

00:51:22.110 --> 00:51:34.980
<v David Boeri>At some point close to midnight, according to the accounts of one of the people in that room, Todashev said, look, I was there, but I didn't do the murders.
<v David Boeri>在午夜前后的某个时间，根据房间里某人的陈述，托达舍夫说，看，我当时在场，但我没有参与谋杀。

00:51:34.980 --> 00:52:06.780
<v Susan Zalkind>This, according to David's sources, was Ibragim's confession. If it's true, that means Ibragim was, at the very least, an accessory to murder. David says one of the state troopers went out of the room to make a phone call back to Massachusetts. The other two interviewers were left in the room with Ibragim as he was about to write down his confession. According to David's sources, the trooper in the room sent a text message to the FBI agent right next to him that Ibragim looked like he was going to go off.
<v Susan Zalkind>这是根据大卫消息来源的易卜拉欣的供词。如果是真的，那就意味着易卜拉欣至少是谋杀的从犯。大卫说其中一名州警走出房间去拨打回马萨诸塞州的电话。另外两名审问者留在房间里陪着即将写下供词的易卜拉欣。根据大卫消息来源，留在房间里的警察给身边的FBI探员发了一条短信，说易卜拉欣看起来要失控了。

00:52:06.780 --> 00:52:37.610
<v David Boeri>And it is at that point that Todashev is said to have flipped the table and gone after the FBI agent. The trooper says he never saw a human move as fast as Todashev did. The trooper also says that Todashev picked up a pipe, that he came at the trooper, and that he would have smashed his skull with the pipe if the FBI agent hadn't shot him.
<v David Boeri>就在那时，据说托达舍夫掀翻了桌子，扑向了FBI探员。警察说他从未见过人的动作像托达舍夫那样快。警察还说，托达舍夫捡起一根管子，他冲向警察，如果FBI探员没开枪打死他，他就会用管子敲碎他的头骨。

00:52:37.610 --> 00:53:00.650
<v David Boeri>Todashev comes over the table. The FBI agent, recovering, fires three shots. Todashev went down to his knee and then got up. And then the second round of four bullets was fired by the FBI agent, and he went down and stayed down.
<v David Boeri>托达舍夫翻过桌子。FBI探员在恢复后开了三枪。托达舍夫跪倒在地，然后又站了起来。接着FBI探员开了第二轮四发子弹，他倒下后就没再起来。

00:53:00.650 --> 00:53:29.190
<v Susan Zalkind>From all the stories I've heard about Ibragim's temper, it seems very possible that he could have lunged or lashed out in a frustrating situation, and that an agent could have felt threatened by it. But I also want to point out that, from my reporting, it's perfectly possible Ibragim could have blown up at an agent in that situation even if he was completely innocent of the murders. His friend Khusen told TV reporters that the day of that final interview, Ibragim told him he was afraid agents were going to frame him for something.
<v Susan Zalkind>从我听到的所有关于易卜拉欣脾气的故事来看，他完全可能在令人沮丧的情况下冲过来或发火，而探员可能对此感到威胁。但我也想指出，根据我的报道，完全可能即使易卜拉欣是谋杀案的局外人，他也会在那种情况下对探员爆发。他的朋友库森告诉电视记者，那次最终审讯的当天，易卜拉欣告诉他，他害怕探员会给他设局。

00:53:29.190 --> 00:53:38.760
<v Khusen Taramov>He felt. He had a bad feeling, because he felt like there was going to be setup. He felt like there was going to be setup, bad setup against him, you know what I mean?
<v Khusen Taramov>他感觉到了。他有种不祥的预感，因为他觉得会有个套。他觉得会有个套，对他很坏的套，你知道我意思吗？

00:53:38.760 --> 00:53:51.650
<v Khusen Taramov>Because he felt-- he told me, like, they're making up such a crazy stuff. I don't know why they're doing it. Why are they doing it? OK, I'm answering the questions, but they're still making up some connection, some crazy stuff. I don't know why they're doing it.
<v Khusen Taramov>因为他感觉——他告诉我，他们编造了那么多疯狂的事。我不知道他们为什么要这样做。为什么要这样做？好吧，我在回答问题，但他们仍在编造一些联系，一些疯狂的东西。我不知道他们为什么要这样做。

00:53:51.650 --> 00:54:10.430
<v Susan Zalkind>Five hours of intense accusations for a murder he knew he didn't commit? That could make Ibragim explode too.
<v Susan Zalkind>对于一起他知道自己没参与的谋杀，连续五个小时的强烈指责？那也可能让易卜拉欣爆炸。

00:54:10.430 --> 00:54:23.150
<v Susan Zalkind>Ibrahim's wife told me that FBI agents told her that they had DNA evidence against Ibragim. But if that was the case, why didn't they arrest him? Did they not have enough evidence for an indictment?
<v Susan Zalkind>易卜拉欣的妻子告诉我，FBI探员告诉她他们有针对易卜拉欣的DNA证据。但如果情况如此，他们为什么不逮捕他？他们没有足够的证据提起指控吗？

00:54:23.150 --> 00:54:48.320
<v Susan Zalkind>I believe that the night of that final interview, any evidence they had against Ibragim was thin. I believe that because they didn't arrest him, and because many people told me that the FBI kept investigating Ibragim after he was dead. And yes, the FBI might have kept interviewing people even if they had tons of evidence already, but it almost seems like they were starting an investigation into Ibragim from scratch.
<v Susan Zalkind>我相信在那次最终审讯的那晚，他们拥有的针对易卜拉欣的任何证据都很薄弱。我相信这一点，因为他们没有逮捕他，而且许多人告诉我，FBI在易卜拉欣死后一直在调查他。是的，即使他们已经有了大量证据，FBI可能还会继续询问人们，但这几乎看起来像是在从零开始调查易卜拉欣。

00:54:48.320 --> 00:55:16.930
<v Susan Zalkind>Employees at MMA gyms, Ibragim's former roommate, people who worked in the hookah bar plaza, people far from the murder, they all told me the FBI came around asking very basic questions about Ibragim after his death. Lawyers at CARE, the Council on American Islamic Relations, told me in the weeks after Ibragim was killed they were getting frantic calls from Ibragim's acquaintances around the country saying the FBI was trying to talk to them.
<v Susan Zalkind>健身房的员工、易卜拉欣的前室友、在水烟吧广场工作的人、离谋杀案很远的人，他们都告诉我，易卜拉欣死后，FBI过来问一些关于他的非常基础的问题。美国伊斯兰关系理事会（CARE）的律师告诉我，在易卜拉欣被杀后的几周里，他们接到了来自全国各地易卜拉欣熟人的疯狂电话，说FBI正试图和他们谈话。

00:55:16.930 --> 00:55:35.870
<v Susan Zalkind>When the FBI did manage to get Ashur into an interview room four months later, this guy who'd never been to Boston, who'd only known him Ibragim for a year, they grilled him for hours about Ibragim and the Waltham murders, murders that, allegedly, Ibragim had already confessed to being involved in.
<v Susan Zalkind>四个月后，当FBI确实设法把阿舒尔带进审讯室时，这个从未去过波士顿、只认识易卜拉欣一年的家伙，他们盘问了他几个小时关于易卜拉欣和沃尔瑟姆谋杀案的事，而据称易卜拉欣已经供认参与了那些谋杀。

00:55:35.870 --> 00:56:16.540
<v Susan Zalkind>One reasonable explanation for all these interviews, and for the FBI and Massachusetts investigators to keep the murder case open, is that they think maybe there are other accomplices out there. But I believe there's still a lot about this case they don't know. We may never find out who killed those three men, or why they were killed. If Ibragim were still alive, we may have had a better chance to get to the bottom of this. But instead, the FBI and the Massachusetts State Police decided to interrogate a trained fighter with a hair trigger temper in his own overheated apartment for five hours.
<v Susan Zalkind>关于所有这些审讯，以及FBI和马萨诸塞州调查人员保持谋杀案调查的开放状态，一种合理的解释是他们认为可能还有其他同伙在外面。但我相信关于这个案子他们仍有很多未知。我们可能永远不会知道是谁杀害了那三个人，或者他们为什么要被杀。如果易卜拉欣还活着，我们可能有更好的机会查明真相。但相反，FBI和马萨诸塞州警察决定在一个闷热的公寓里，审讯一名性子急躁的格斗选手五个小时。

00:56:16.540 --> 00:56:32.680
